# Copy to `android/key.properties` and fill in. That file is git-ignored and
# must stay that way — it holds the passwords to the publishing key.
#
# CREATING THE KEYSTORE (once, ever):
#
#   keytool -genkey -v -keystore ~/Keys/council-release.jks \
#     -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10000 -alias council
#
# keytool prompts for the two passwords; nothing writes them down for you. Put
# the keystore outside this repository — anywhere inside it is one `git add -A`
# away from being published.
#
# THEN BACK IT UP, somewhere that is not this machine. Android will not install
# an update signed with a different key than the installed copy, so losing this
# file strands every reader who ever installed Council: no re-signing, no
# recovery, nothing to do but ask them to uninstall and start again. It is the
# single least replaceable file in the project.
#
# VERIFYING A BUILD is signed with it:
#
#   apksigner verify --print-certs build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-release.apk
#
# The SHA-256 fingerprint it prints must match every release, forever. Record it
# alongside the backup so a future build can be checked against it rather than
# against memory.

storeFile=/Users/you/Keys/council-release.jks
storePassword=
keyAlias=council
keyPassword=
